This page contains reading notes, not derivations. It lists four sources with short German summaries. Nothing is computed, so there is nothing to reconstruct mathematically. The entries are:

(1) Berliner Monatsberichte, session of 17 June 1852. Eisenstein, on comparing ternary quadratic forms that have different determinants. Only the title is recorded.

(2) Poggendorff's Annalen (P.A.) vol. 87, no. 1, 1852, taken from Müller's Archiv für Anatomie und Physiologie 1852. Helmholtz, 'Über die Theorie der zusammengesetzten Farben' (on the theory of compound colours). The summary covers:
- the difference between compounding colours and compounding tones;
- the history of the subject;
- mixing spectral colours;
- mixing pigment colours (a) with a colour top and (b) by superposing the mirror image of a coloured wafer (Oblate) onto a differently coloured one, giving the main result Blue + Yellow = White;
- the explanation that mixing pigments gives green, because the mixture passes only the rays transmitted by both the blue and the yellow transparent bodies.
In modern terms this is the distinction between additive and subtractive colour mixing.

(3) P.A. vol. 86, no. 4, 1852. Helmholtz, on Brewster's 'new analysis of sunlight'. Brewster held that every homogeneous (monochromatic) ray is a mixture of red, yellow and blue of equal refrangibility. He based this on apparent colour changes of homogeneous light after absorption in coloured media. Helmholtz attributes those changes to three causes:
- diffuse stray light;
- physiological contrast, i.e. induced complementary colour impressions;
- the fact that the same homogeneous light gives slightly different colour impressions at different intensities (at dazzling brightness all colours become white; in modern terms, Bezold–Brücke-type effects and saturation).
He shows that when these sources of error are avoided, no change of colour is seen.

(4) Liouville's Journal de mathématiques pures et appliquées, 1852 (dated 'Juillet' 1852 on the page, with 'Novembre' written above). Ossian Bonnet, 'Sur le développement des fonctions en séries ordonnées suivant les fonctions X_n et Y_n'. These are expansions in Legendre polynomials and Laplace (spherical-harmonic) functions. Cross-references:
- Poisson, Journal de l'École Polytechnique, cahier 19; Connaissance des Temps 1829 and 1831; Théorie mathématique de la chaleur, p. 212;
- Lejeune-Dirichlet, Crelle vol. 17 (the 1837 convergence proof for Laplace series).
The content of Bonnet's paper is summarised on the next page (Abel's theorem, convergence proof, asymptotic formula for X_n).
